ed the favorite to defend its crown at the 2000 Summer Olympics. Challenging this nation would be France, who finished in second (1997 and 1998) or third (1999) at the last three World Championships, and Australia, the 1999 runner-up and the 1998 bronze medalist. In the opening round, Australia and France were indeed victorious in their heats, the latter in the round’s best time, but Denmark struggled and finished last in a heat won by Italy.  The Danes survived by winning the repêchage, but lost to the Italians again in the semi-finals, albeit not by anywhere near as large of a margin. Australia and France, meanwhile, met in this round and had an exhilarating race, with the former edging the latter out by a mere 0.03 seconds at the finish line. In the final, however, it was France who gained the upper hand and won by a slightly more comfortable 0.41 seconds, leaving the Australians with silver. Denmark, meanwhile, finally managed to get the best of the Italians, but not by much, capturing bronze by a margin of only 0.26 seconds."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} "France"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "8"} [:a {:href "/athletes/92550"} "Laurent Porchier"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/92541"} "Jean-Christophe Bette"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/92547"} "Yves Hocdé"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/36932"} "Xavier Dorfman"]]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} "Australia"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUS.png"